Historical evidence recorded before the skeleton migration. Counts, pending statuses, temporary handles and next-step instructions describe that checkpoint; consult the current development Record before acting.

The fifth engineering block is installed after `@engineering-material-review`. Eight new lessons and two preserved power-engineering Records form this exact continuation: `engineering-electrical-function`, `engineering-node-model`, `pe-circuits`, `engineering-port-equivalent`, `engineering-electrical-limits`, `pe-operating-state`, `engineering-electrical-bounds`, `engineering-electrical-observation`, `engineering-electric-study`, `engineering-electric-review`. Engineering now has **55 common learning Records**. Shared circuit/operating-state Records retain original identities, imports, prose, source assignments and specialist routes; bounded common reading notes and separate edges were appended. Already selected circuit physics, engineering network/realization reasoning and fifth linear mathematics are contextual reuse. The broader source-equivalent Record is contextual, preserving its generator/converter scope.

The original model has a positive ideal DC source E, source resistance R_s, common connection R_c, two feed branches r₁/r₂, resistive loads L₁/L₂ and a declared ideal return. With r=R_s+R_c, feed v₀ and load terminals v₁,v₂, charge balance and linear response produce a three-node conductance system. Its positive quadratic form proves uniqueness. An independent A=r₁+L₁, B=r₂+L₂, R_p=AB/(A+B) reduction recovers every voltage/current. The full power identity is EI=I²R_s+I²R_c+i₁²r₁+i₂²r₂+v₁²/L₁+v₂²/L₂. Accessible source-terminal voltage V_t=E−IR_s distinguishes ideal-source power from power crossing the source's external boundary. Stored/dynamic, inductive, active-control, changing-temperature and fault behavior remain outside this steady model.

At the L₁ port, E_th=EB/(r+B), R_th=r₁+rB/(r+B) and v₁=E_th−R_th j are derived directly for an arbitrary attached current j. The port relation preserves terminal behavior, not original internal dissipation. The algebraic square inequality proves P_L≤E_th²/(4R_th), with equality at L=R_th; maximum-power matching is distinguished from the stated voltage/current/loss requirements and full-system useful-power fraction. This also supplies a counterexample to blindly using box corners for arbitrary component-power maxima.

The three hypothetical parameter tuples, ordered (E,R_s,R_c,r₁,r₂,L₁,L₂), are A=(12,1,1,2,1,10,5), B=(12,1/2,1/2,1,1/2,10,5), C=(12,1/2,1/4,1/2,1/4,10,5), with volts/ohms as appropriate. A gives both load voltages 20/3 V, I=2 A and useful fraction 5/9. B gives 60/7 V, I=18/7 A and fraction 5/7. C gives 160/17 V, I=48/17 A and fraction 40/51. Useful power is defined as electrical dissipation in the intended resistive loads; no actual thermal function is validated. Decreasing connection resistance raises load voltage and source current, so source dissipation can rise while connection losses fall.

Invented screening requirements are load voltages in [8,10.5] V, source current≤3.4 A and losses in (R_s,R_c,r₁,r₂)≤(6,4,2,3) W. B and C pass nominal screening. E then varies ±5%, every resistance ±10%, with all Cartesian-box combinations admitted and no statistical distribution assumed. Monotonic derivatives/positive rational forms locate exact continuous-box load-voltage and current extrema. Powers use the valid conservative product R_max I_max²; a failed sufficient power bound is not treated as proof of an actual violation.

B has actual violating voltage corners, approximately [7.6314,9.5126] and [7.6440,9.5029] V. C has exact voltage bounds [258552/30347,426888/41467] and [258552/30325,426888/41485] V, within [8,10.5]. C's maximum current tuple is (56/17,616/549,1232/555) A. Its conservative loss bounds are (8624/1445,4312/1445,1043504/1507005,2087008/1540125) W, each within the respective limit. The rational comparisons establish C's model screen over the continuous box. They do not establish real ratings, environmental/duty suitability, geometry, cost, service life or installation qualification.

The observation study proves that all downstream values depend on R_s,R_c only through their sum. B's rival(1/4,3/4) Ω preserves downstream values but changes source/connection losses. An added V_t observation, with E and I independently established at the same state, identifies R_s=(E−V_t)/I and R_c=(V_t−v₀)/I. B has V_t=75/7 V; its rival 159/14 V. A hypothetical 100 Ω meter across L₁ instead changes the system: B's loaded (v₀,v₁,v₂)=(3663/391,3300/391,3330/391) V and I=1029/391 A. Meter power remains separate from useful load power. No actual instrument observation was performed.

Original embedded `realization_study.py` and `realization_checks.py` reuse the unchanged `linear_space_study.py`. They passed **41,299 exact checks** through Nix/Python 3.14.8: **128 independently reduced network cases**, **4, 374 range-grid cases** (3⁷ points for each of B/C) and **nine meter cases**, plus nominal, port/matched-power, ambiguity and invalid-domain checks. Independent reduction, direct substitution, power balance and analytically selected corner extrema check the shared-elimination calculation. Monotonicity and conservative-product proofs supply the continuous-set guarantee; finite grids are implementation evidence. All three installed programs were extracted to a separate directory and the same suite passed.

